language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ equal_sized_groups: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ I have a sorted list of N elements, and I want to divide them into K groups, by choosing K-1 split points between them. There may be elements with the same value, and it is necessary to have items with same value in the same group. Find K groups as close in size to round(N/K) as possible. Each group holds at least one element. The break points are the numbers of elements before each split point, and the total error (the sum over the groups of the absolute difference between the group size and round(N/K)) is minimised.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `equal_sized_groups.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/equal_sized_groups.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `equal_sized_groups_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py equal_sized_groups.json
$   savilerow model.eprime equal_sized_groups_1.param -run-solver

$ N: the number of elements in the sorted list (derived from the data; not a field of the reader's JSON)
given a_dim1 : int
letting N = a_dim1
$ a[i]: the i-th element of the sorted list, indices 1-based (the data's index 0 is a[1])
given a : matrix indexed by [int(1..N)] of int
$ k: the number of groups
given k : int
$ target: round(N/k), with halves rounded up
letting target = (2 * N + k) / (2 * k)

$ x[j]: the break point j, the number of elements before it, for j = 1 to k-1 (the output, indices 1-based)
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..k-1)] of int(1..N-1)
$ ends[j]: the number of elements in the first j groups, so ends[0] = 0 and ends[k] = N
find ends : matrix indexed by [int(0..k)] of int(0..N)
$ total_error: the sum over the groups of the distance between the group size and target
find total_error : int(0..2 * N)

branching on [x]
minimising total_error
such that
$ the first group starts at the first element
ends[0] = 0,
$ the last group ends at the last element
ends[k] = N,
$ break point j is the end of group j, so each group is a run of consecutive elements
forAll j : int(1..k-1) . x[j] = ends[j],
$ each group holds at least one element, so the group ends strictly increase
forAll j : int(1..k) . ends[j] > ends[j - 1],
$ a break point cannot split elements with the same value: the elements either side of it differ
forAll j : int(1..k-1) . a[x[j]] != a[x[j] + 1],
$ the total error is the sum over the groups of the distance between each group size and round(N/k)
total_error = (sum j : int(1..k) . |(ends[j] - ends[j - 1]) - target|)
